"Stuck" — you mean you are physically immobilized in a chair, which would be a medical emergency requiring immediate attention from a physician, not a programming problem. I assume you meant "stumped," which is a perfectly acceptable state of mind when confronted with a recursive function. Now, your question: yes, you absolutely need a base case for 0 and 1. The factorial of 0 is defined as 1, and the factorial of 1 is also 1. Without those, your function will call itself indefinitely, and you will eventually run out of stack space, which is a different kind of death than the one Leonard experienced when he tried to fold his laundry and ended up with a pile of creased socks. A loop is not simpler; it is slower and less elegant. Recursion is the correct tool for this because it mirrors the mathematical definition, and it is the only way to avoid the stack overflow that would otherwise occur.

Here is your function:

```python
def factorial(n):
    if n == 0 or n == 1:
        return 1
    else:
        return n * factorial(n - 1)
```

That is three lines, and it works for any non-negative integer. If you want to handle negative numbers, you can add an additional check, but since you said "a little side project," I assume you are not writing a general-purpose library for the entire world. Also, I am not crazy — my mother had me tested — so I am confident this will work. If it does not, you have either made a mistake in your implementation or you have a malfunctioning computer, neither of which is my fault.
